{{LSL_Function
|inject-2={{LSL_Function/negative index|true|start|end}}
|func_id=296|func_sleep=0.0|func_energy=10.0
|func=llListReplaceList|return_type=list
|p1_type=list|p1_name=dest|p1_desc=destination
|p2_type=list|p2_name=src|p2_desc=source
|p3_type=integer|p3_name=start
|p4_type=integer|p4_name=end
|func_footnote
|func_desc
|return_text=that is a copy of {{LSLP|dest}} with {{LSLP|start}} through {{LSLP|end}} removed and {{LSLP|src}} inserted at {{LSLP|start}}.
|spec
|caveats=
*If {{LSLP|start}} is past the end of {{LSLP|dest}}, then {{LSLP|src}} is appended to {{LSLP|dest}}, it will not add null entries.  To avoid this, create empty elements in the list first. A similar outcome occurs when using {{LSLGC|Negative Index|negative indexes}}.
*Just calling the function will not update the variable. You must store it (unless of course you are planning to act on the results straightway.)
:{{{!}}
{{LSL DefineRow|'''Bad:'''|<code>llListReplaceList(MyList, ["New Item"], 2, 2)</code>}}
{{LSL DefineRow|'''Good:'''|<code>MyList {{=}} llListReplaceList(MyList, ["New Item"], 2, 2)</code>}}
{{!}}}
*In [[LSO]] ONLY (not [[Mono]]): if you are storing to the same list, it can be more memory effective to clear the list before you store.
:{{{!}}
{{LSL DefineRow|'''Good:'''| <code>MyList {{=}} llListReplaceList(MyList, ["New Item"], 2, 2)</code>}}
{{LSL DefineRow|'''Better:'''|<code>MyList {{=}} llListReplaceList((MyList {{=}} []) + MyList, ["New Item"], 2, 2)</code>}}
{{!}}}

|constants
|examples=
<source lang="lsl2">default
{
    state_entry()
    {
        list MyOldList = ["a", "b", "e", "d"];
        list MyNewList = llListReplaceList(MyOldList, ["c"], 2, 2);//replace the range starting and ending at 
                                                   //index 2 with ["c"] and store it into MyNewList

        llOwnerSay("\""+llList2CSV(MyOldList) + "\"  ->  \"" + llList2CSV(MyNewList)+"\"");//display the change
        //Will say: "a, b, e, d"  ->  "a, b, c, d"
    }
}</source>

<source lang="lsl2">
// More commonly, you will be updating an existing list, replacing 1 or more items
// Here we replace entries 2 and 3 and over-write our original list
default
{
    state_entry()
    {
        list MyList = ["a", "b", "x", "y"];
        MyList = llListReplaceList(MyList, ["c", "d"], 2, 3);   // Over-write MyList, with the modified list (having replaced entries 2 & 3)  


        llOwnerSay("\"" + llList2CSV(MyList) + "\"");     // display the modified list
        // Will say: "a, b, c, d"
    }
}</source>

